c  from a  a bird's eye view you know recovering  from errors is a really critical  import a critical thing that  laboratories do  and i my experience is that the practice  varies  widely and again and that's for good  reason right there's no one best way to  do this  i know you've written about this and you  have some deep experience could you just  start us off by explaining what your  sort of philosophy is  yes i'd be happy to uh so i  i think if you look at the field of  laboratory qc we spend  a huge amount of time figuring out what  rules to use  what qc material to use how to set our  means in sds  and when and basically i think from a  bench staff  standpoint uh you just want the qc to be  in  you wanna you want it to be in don't  wreck my day  but the fact is when qc is out  there's more to worry about than just  getting qc  back in and i think we have to remember  that qc  is our surrogate for telling us that the  patient results we're reporting are  accurate and that's  why we do that's why we invest all of  this time into setting up  our qc strategies and our materials and  our means  so when that qc is out we need i think  our  our gut reaction shouldn't just be  get the qc back in our gut reaction  should be  what's happening with our patient  results what did we report that was  inaccurate is there harm is there  patient  impact so i think you know 30 000 foot  view  philosophically is to really remember  what qc failures reflect  and they reflect that your patients are  at risk your patient results are at risk  so i think the and i i'll echo  also what dr straszewski said earlier  about having  sops i think that you need to  have it completely laid out how you're  going to react  to the patient impact portion of acute  failure  before it ever happens you don't want to  be doing this on the fly so i call this  the recovery phase  and it really needs to be laid out so i  think the steps i always kind of lay it  out in four steps and they're very  simple steps  um you know first is the qc failure real  and by by real i say just the common  sense things make sure  it wasn't the wrong level of material or  levels were swapped or  something else was going on but that  doesn't mean keep repeating the qc over  and over  because if you've set up your rules  properly you're detecting a  real problem second contain it so make  sure you're not  continuing to report off that analyzer  if you truly have an out of control  condition  it is important enough that you stop  testing hopefully you have a redundant  analyzer  but either way contain it and then of  course you want to fix  you want to fix the assay you want to  recalibrate or  change the reagent pack whatever the  problem was if there's a problem with a  pipe header or a tubing  you want to fix that but hopefully you  have redundancy in your lab that you can  start considering a patient impact as  well  so in our laboratory there are again  numerous approaches to this  but you have your mindset should really  be that  from that out of control condition that  you've just detected  the patient's results that you reported  are at risk of being incorrect  from that point back to whenever your  last  good qc was so you need to  actually repeat those patient samples  repeat test them on an analyzer that is  in control  now if you're a small lab low volumes  and maybe you've only run  20 or 30 patients since the last qc  event  you can re-measure all of them and do  that comparison and see whether you  reported anything erroneously  i come from a very high volume high  throughput automated lab  we go back in batches of 10 patients  that's not a magic number by any means  it's a number that  is practical and it works for us  but then what do you do so you're now  repeating patient  samples of results that in our lab we  report in real time there's auto  verification  so these results have already been  reported and that's why i think  recovery processes need to be set out  beforehand because it has to be  immediate you don't want to catch this  the next day  and say oh my gosh all of the tshs that  we reported yesterday  are at risk so it's immediate  and but we what we also have our  criteria  set for what constitutes needing to  revise a result  because if if you reported a tsh i'll  use tsh as an example a tsh of  five and when you repeat it it's  4.8 that's really  within the imprecision of the assay so  what we also have  in we in our lab we call it our revision  limits  but these are what kind of differences  constitute  needing to correct a result so we will  go back in batches of 10  we will compare the original reported  result with the result that we're now  repeating  on an in-control analyzer and we compare  those results and if it  exceeds our pre-established criteria we  will correct that result  so that's our that's my very simplistic  you know four-step approach  i think um a few of the caveats  are you know if you're dealing with  analytes where  normal is undetectable and your last 10  results were undetectable  keep going back until you have  measurable values so there needs to be  some common sense  incorporated if you have analytes that  are  intrinsically unstable and can't be  repeated that also needs to be built  into  your recovery plan  so that's how we approach it um  and i i'm happy to elaborate but i also  want to give others a chance to chime in  yeah excellent um uh you know i i want  to  empha i want to emphasize that the need  for that sop  on um what steps you take after  this type of discovery um i'll i'll  confess that we recent here at tri-core  we recently had a large-scale testing  error that qc  would not have detected because it  wasn't it was a manual  it was the instruments were fine it was  just how  some manual programming of the analyzer  was occurring when when our track was  being replaced  and um when it happened and we  recognized that we didn't have  an sop to follow and i was doing exactly  what you just said not to do dr bauman  was  trying to fix this on the fly uh it  it was very difficult and it took longer  than it should have  so uh we're now working with our quality  and safety teams to create an sop that  will help guide us because it is a lot  to do  when you have to go back you know if  you're if you're trying to change one or  two results it's fairly easy um  but when you've got a lot it becomes uh  much more complicated  um and before we go into the next topic  there was a question that came in that's  that's relevant and and dr bauman you  already addressed it a little bit  but it's essentially um what do you what  what's the best practice  when you might have a a qc failure  but the um the the error is still within  what what many would be the total  allowable error  um you know do you have to correct those  patient results and  you already touched on that because you  guys have developed revision limits for  your various tests and if the repeat  result is within that limit  there's no correction needed can you  just touch base on how you  develop those revision limits  yes and um it is something  it's a lot of effort to go through your  whole test menu and establish those  but it's so worth it when things go  wrong so we do  we actually use allowable total error  we do look at um clia  recommended differences as well we kind  of compile  all of the sources of evidence that  there are for these things  and then we we look at the imprecision  of the assay  i would say the main driver we use is  allowable total error i've been  toying with the idea of using reference  change values  as that amount of significant change  that may be a  nice tool to use as well  but mostly allowable total error and  you're right in so an assay like ast  where the imprecision is excellent we're  at  cvs of you know one to two percent  and yet the allowable total error is  more in the  15 to 20 percent when we have a qc  failure we hardly ever revise patient  results and that's  okay that's what we want we then have  analytes like calcium  which is notoriously imprecise  for how it's used clinically and when we  have  a 22s failure for calcium  we often are revising several results so  it will depend um we've we have some  strategies in the lab where we're trying  to  to optimize this but we're  very limited time if anyone's interested  they can reach out to me i can explain  more but  in general that's how we do it  great thank you um before i go on to the  next theme just uh  dr savinthia straszewski anything else  to add to the  recovery okay all right let's move on  um let's talk about qualitative qc you  know much of what we're talking about  has been very focused on quantitative qc  qualitative qc is certainly important  and it's out there  um and so i'm going to ask um i guess dr  savinsky you want to get us started  what's the best practice to evaluate  qualitative qc  uh do you just look at the qualitative  result  or do you uh many times you get a  numeric result  that the qualitative interpretation is  based on so what do you all do  yeah so not only best practice depending  on your accrediting agency you may have  to  track that and treat it as you would a  regular quantitative qc so we do monitor  the instrument signal single to noise  cut  off signal to calibrate or cut off  whatever assay term you use  as we would monitor any quantitative qc  and i think that the natural idea and  the reason behind that  is that when you have a qualitative  assay that is going to be positive or  negative  at a certain threshold and your  qc is right above that threshold if it  were to go negative  okay but let's suppose you have a  threshold of one  and anything above one is considered  positive and your qc is running at a  hundred with an sd of two  you know if you're not monitoring that  shift  and your 100 suddenly becomes 50.  you have to wonder what's happening to  those patients that are right around two  are t